NNAAMMEE
     mmrrssaass — LSI MegaRAID 6Gb/s and 12Gb/s SAS+SATA RAID controller driver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following lines in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ddeevviiccee ppccii
           ddeevviiccee mmrrssaass

     Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
     following line in loader.conf(5):

           mrsas_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmrrssaass driver will detect LSI's next generation (6Gb/s and 12Gb/s) PCI
     Express SAS/SATA RAID controllers.  See the HHAARRDDWWAARREE section for the sup‐
     ported devices list.  A disk (virtual disk/physical disk) attached to the
     mmrrssaass driver will be visible to the user through camcontrol(8) as
     _/_d_e_v_/_d_a_? device nodes.  A simple management interface is also provided on
     a per-controller basis via the _/_d_e_v_/_m_r_s_a_s_? device node.

     The mmrrssaass name is derived from the phrase "MegaRAID SAS HBA", which is
     substantially different than the old "MegaRAID" Driver mfi(4) which does
     not connect targets to the cam(4) layer and thus requires a new driver
     which attaches targets to the cam(4) layer.  Older MegaRAID controllers
     are supported by mfi(4) and will not work with mmrrssaass, but both the mfi(4)
     and mmrrssaass drivers can detect and manage the LSI MegaRAID SAS
     2208/2308/3008/3108 series of controllers.

     The device.hints(5) option is provided to tune the mmrrssaass driver's behav‐
     ior for LSI MegaRAID SAS 2208/2308/3008/3108 controllers.  By default,
     the mfi(4) driver will detect these controllers.  See the PPRRIIOORRIITTYY sec‐
     tion to know more about driver priority for MR-Fusion devices.

     mmrrssaass will provide a priority of (-30) (between BUS_PROBE_DEFAULT and
     BUS_PROBE_LOW_PRIORITY) at probe call for device id's 0x005B, 0x005D, and
     0x005F so that mmrrssaass does not take control of these devices without user
     intervention.

     Solid-state drives (SSD) get ATA TRIM support with mmrrssaass if underlying
     adapter allows it.  This may require configuring SSD as Non-RAID drive
     rather then JBOD virtual mode.

